          .proof
            %h6#proof_16 Proof
            %p
              It is clear that the morphism of chain complexes is an objectwise surjection and hence maps to a projective fibration under
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Ξ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Xi
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mo ♭
                      %msup
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi B
                        %mi n
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{\flat} \mathbf{B}^n U(1)
            %p
              By the discussion at
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-cohesive+site"} ∞-cohesive site
              we have that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mo ♭
                    %mo =
                    %mi Disc
                    %mi Γ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{\flat} = Disc \Gamma
              is given on fibrant objects by first evaluating on the point and then extending back to a constant simplicial presheaf. By the
              %a{:href => "#CircleLienGroup"} above discussion
              we have that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ξ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Xi (U(1)[n])
              is indeed fibrant, and so a fibrant presentation of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mo ♭
                    %msup
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi B
                      %mi n
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{\flat} \mathbf{B}^n U(1)
              is given by the
              %em constant
              presheaf
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mi const
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo :
                      %mi U
                      %mo ↦
                      %mi Ξ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(1)_{const} [n] : U \mapsto \Xi(U(1)[n])
            %p
              The inclusion
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi const
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo →
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(1)_{const}[n] \to U(1)[n]
              is not yet a fibration. But by a basic fact of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+sheaf+cohomology"} abelian sheaf cohomology
              – using the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poincare+lemma"} Poincare lemma
              – we have a global weak equivalence
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi n
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mi const
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mo ≃
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
